// `h.run(21)` parses as a call into a module named `h`, which does not exist. The diagnostic for that
// used to offer one repair, `run(h, ...)` -- correct when `run` is a free function taking `h`, and
// impossible when `run` is a *field* of `h` holding a function value, which is this case. The repair
// that works is to bind the field and call it. The message names both now, because this runs before type
// checking and so cannot tell which case it is looking at; the reader can. Letting the call work
// directly is C15.
struct Handler {
    name: string
    run: fn(int) -> int
}

fn main() {
    val h = Handler { name: "double", run: fn(n: int) -> int { return n * 2 } }
    print(h.run(21))
}
